    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nFixes the `(L4) Plugin Integration` CI job, where the three offline tests in `plugins_tests/test_colbert_query_io_processor_plugins.py` fail with `Free memory on device cuda:0 (20.25/22.03 GiB) on startup is less than desired GPU memory utilization (0.92, 20.27 GiB)` (e.g. full builds [95829](https://buildkite.com/vllm/ci/builds/95829) and [95887](https://buildkite.com/vllm/ci/builds/95887); the same job passes intermittently in other builds).\n\n## Claims\n\n- The offline ColBERT plugin tests no longer depend on a ~20 MB margin to start their engine while the module-scoped server is running.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pytest -q tests/plugins_tests/test_colbert_query_io_processor_plugins.py` on 1x GB300: 7 passed.\n- I could not reproduce the L4 failure locally: on a 280 GB device the server's ~1.8 GB footprint stays far below the 8% headroom. The fix rests on the numbers in the CI log above. The offline engine now needs 50% of the device free instead of 92%, about 11 GiB on L4, while about 20 GiB is free.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `server` fixture is module-scoped, so its engine is only torn down at the end of the module and is still holding about 1.8 GB when the offline tests start. Each offline `vllm_runner` used the default `gpu_memory_utilization=0.92`, which on a 22 GiB L4 requires 20.27 GiB free. With the server alive, 20.25 GiB was free, so whether the engine could start came down to a few MB of allocator noise. ColBERT is a small pooling model and does not need a large KV budget, so the offline runners now use `gpu_memory_utilization=0.5`. The server keeps its default.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\nNot a duplicate: no open PR touches this test file or its memory settings.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
